add(5,'What the system had to handle','Singapore',30,
'The planner, online state and physical drivetrain have different kinds of limits.','limits',{'type':'limits'},
['**Local observation** of moving agents','**Soft penalties** in the nominal plan','**Online clipping** before wheel conversion'],
"I had local agent observations, a bounded arena, and a robot that couldn't move sideways. The offline plan used soft speed and acceleration penalties. Online, I clipped internal acceleration and velocity. These mechanisms are different, and they don't prove universal bounds on physical motion.",
"I organized the system into an offline plan and an online correction.",[
 d('Perception radius','What does the 0.60 m radius mean?','In the physical adapter, moving-agent influence is limited using edge-to-edge clearance of 0.60 m.',['The camera can see more than the corrector uses.','A global sensor does not make the decision rule nonlocal.'],eq('d = ‖x − c‖ − r_agent − r_robot\nF_mobile = 0 when d ≥ 0.60 m','Physical adapter cutoff'),'ADAPT P',locator='compute_phase5bis_realtime_command; draft Table 6'),
 d('Speed / acceleration','Why are offline and online numbers different?','The draft uses 0.35 m/s and 0.8 m/s² as offline penalty thresholds, and 0.25 m/s and 1.0 m/s² for online clipping.',['They belong to different stages.','A penalty threshold is not a hard bound.'],table(['Stage','Speed','Acceleration'],[['Offline penalties','0.35 m/s','0.8 m/s²'],['Online state clipping','0.25 m/s','1.0 m/s²']],'Draft Tables 3 and 6'),'P REAL',locator='Draft Tables 3/6; NewtonEulerRealConfig'),
 d('Hardware limits','Were those physical bounds verified for all motion?','No. I verified the command logic and measured the drivetrain, but did not establish a universal physical envelope.',['The command-to-motion response is imperfect.','Wheel commands, modeled velocity and observed velocity are distinct.'],img('motor-identification.png','Open-loop drivetrain measurements'),'P REAL',locator='Draft §3.5.3'),
 d('Assumptions','What assumptions matter most?','The testbed is planar, externally localized, relatively slow, and uses marked obstacles and robots.',['Marker visibility and calibrated geometry matter.','The campaign covers a small set of repeatable tasks.'],table(['Assumption','Consequence'],[['Overhead markers','Visibility required'],['Planar arena','No 3D validation'],['Low speed','Limited dynamic scope']]),'P A B',locator='Draft §3.1, §5.5')])

add(6,'The two-layer architecture','Singapore',45,
'The nominal plan handles the known static scene; current observations drive the local correction.','diagram',{'type':'architecture'},
['**Offline:** build a nominal trajectory','**Online:** react to current observations','**Physical loop:** observe → command → measure'],
"The first layer builds a nominal trajectory using the static scene: the start, goal, fixed obstacles, and arena. Moving agents are excluded. The second layer runs during execution. It combines attraction toward the nominal path with repulsion from current observations and damping. I then convert that planar request into wheel commands. The overhead camera closes the loop, and the logs let me inspect positions, forces, commands, and timing afterward. This separation kept the global path problem away from the real-time loop.",
"Before looking inside each layer, here is what I contributed.",[
 d('Full data flow','Where does information travel?','Basler frames reach the Raspberry Pi over the access point and Wi-Fi; Python computes poses and commands; onboard serial drives the motor board.',['The camera is external; computation is carried on the controlled rover.','Agents use their own independent command link.'],flow(['Basler → AP → Wi-Fi','Raspberry Pi: pose + control','USB serial → motor board']),'P A SER',locator='Draft §3.1.3; camera_transport in A summaries'),
 d('Why two layers?','Why split planning and correction?','The static plan provides global direction; the local stage reacts to information that is only available during the run.',['The offline solution cannot include unknown true future motion.','The local method is fast but can still stall.'],table(['Offline plan','Online correction'],[['Known static scene','Current observed scene'],['Global direction','Immediate reaction'],['Local optimum','No global safety guarantee']]),'P CTRL',locator='Draft §2.4, §3.3–3.4'),
 d('Nav2 analogy','Is this a Nav2 implementation?','No. The useful analogy is a planner supplying a reference to a controller. PACO does not use the Nav2 stack.',['Nav2 has planner/controller servers and broader navigation infrastructure.','The analogy explains responsibilities, not software equivalence.'],table(['Role','PACO','Nav2 analogy'],[['Plan','Offline nominal','Planner'],['Follow / react','Local corrector','Controller'],['Integration','Custom Python loop','ROS 2 servers']]),'CTRL NAV2',kind='Conceptual comparison'),
 d('Alternatives','What would you compare against?','I would compare a reactive baseline, periodic replanning and a model-predictive controller under the same sensing and task protocol.',['Use shared success and clearance definitions.','Measure computation and model mismatch too.'],table(['Option','Potential benefit','Cost / risk'],[['Reactive only','No offline solve','Local traps'],['Replanning','Update route','Repeated solve'],['MPC','Explicit prediction / limits','Model + solve budget']]),'P MPC',kind='Proposed comparison')])

